{
 "id": "L5",
 "n": 5,
 "title": "Small talk and likes",
 "goal": "Chat about the weather, say what you like, and say what you're doing right now.",
 "notes": [
  {
   "h": "Liking things: two different words",
   "p": "{{좋아해요}} is an action word, \"to like\". The thing you like takes the object marker {{을}} / {{를}}: {{커피를 좋아해요}}. {{좋아요}} is a describing word, \"is good\". The thing that is good takes the subject marker {{이}} / {{가}}: {{날씨가 좋아요}}. In everyday talk Koreans also say {{커피가 좋아요}} to mean \"I like coffee\", but once the thing takes {{을}} / {{를}} you must use {{좋아해요}}. On its own, {{좋아요}} is also how you accept an invitation: \"sounds good\". The ㅎ in {{좋}} goes silent before a vowel, so {{좋아요}} sounds like \"jo-a-yo\".",
   "ex": [
    [
     "커피를 좋아해요",
     "I like coffee."
    ],
    [
     "날씨가 좋아요",
     "The weather is nice."
    ],
    [
     "좋아요!",
     "Sounds good!"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "h": "The subject marker (i / ga) and the topic marker (eun / neun)",
   "p": "{{이}} / {{가}} points at who or what does something or is something, often new information: {{비가 와요}}, {{날씨가 좋아요}}. Use {{이}} after a consonant and {{가}} after a vowel. {{은}} / {{는}} sets the topic, \"as for …\", and often contrasts: {{오늘은 추워요}} hints that other days are not. A question word that is the subject takes {{이}} / {{가}}, never {{은}} / {{는}}: {{뭐가 맛있어요?}} One special form: {{저}} + {{가}} becomes {{제가}}.",
   "ex": [
    [
     "비가 와요",
     "It's raining."
    ],
    [
     "오늘은 추워요",
     "Today (at least) it's cold."
    ],
    [
     "여기 뭐가 맛있어요?",
     "What's good here?"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "h": "Weather words and the b to w change",
   "p": "Many describing words end in ㅂ: {{덥다}} (hot), {{춥다}} (cold). Before {{어요}}, the ㅂ turns into {{워}}: {{더워요}}, {{추워요}}. You already know two more: {{매워요}} (spicy) and {{가까워요}} (close). Rain and snow \"come\" ({{오다}}): {{비가 와요}}, {{눈이 와요}}. {{따뜻해요}} (warm) and {{시원해요}} (cool) end in {{해요}} and never change.",
   "ex": [
    [
     "오늘 더워요",
     "It's hot today."
    ],
    [
     "추워요",
     "It's cold."
    ],
    [
     "눈이 와요",
     "It's snowing."
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "h": "Doing it right now: -go isseoyo",
   "p": "Take an action word, drop {{다}}, and add {{고 있어요}}: {{마시다}} becomes {{마시고 있어요}}, \"I'm drinking\". Use it for something happening at this moment. Describing words like {{바빠요}} or {{추워요}} never take it. Plain {{뭐 해요?}} can also mean \"what are you doing?\"; {{뭐 하고 있어요?}} stresses \"right now\".",
   "ex": [
    [
     "지금 커피를 마시고 있어요",
     "I'm drinking coffee right now."
    ],
    [
     "지금 뭐 하고 있어요?",
     "What are you doing right now?"
    ],
    [
     "지금 비가 오고 있어요",
     "It's raining right now."
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "h": "Reacting with -neyo, and \"also\" with do",
   "p": "Swap the {{아요}} / {{어요}} ending for {{네요}} when you notice or react to something right now: \"oh, it's …!\". It goes straight onto the stem with no ㅂ change: {{덥다}} becomes {{덥네요}}. Sound point: ㅎ, ㅅ or ㅆ before ㄴ is said as ㄴ, so {{좋네요}} sounds like \"jon-ne-yo\" and {{맛있네요}} like \"ma-sin-ne-yo\". {{도}} means \"also / too\". It takes the place of {{은}} / {{는}}, {{이}} / {{가}} or {{을}} / {{를}} instead of joining them: {{저도 좋아해요}}, {{음악도 좋아해요}}.",
   "ex": [
    [
     "오늘 덥네요!",
     "It's hot today!"
    ],
    [
     "맛있네요!",
     "This is tasty!"
    ],
    [
     "저도 좋아해요",
     "I like it too."
    ]
   ]
  }
 ],
